"A liquid chromatography–tandem mass spectrometric method for quantitative determination of native 5-methyltetrahydrofolate and its polyglutamyl derivatives in raw vegetables (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3850028/)\nciteturn551search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; Quantitative polyglutamyl 5-methyltetrahydrofolate profiles were determined for 18 vegetables from Brassicaceae, Asteraceae and Amaranthaceae of which most have not been analyzed previously and are considered good to excellent sources of dietary folate according to the USDA database [[ref-type: bibr] 24]. To the best of our knowledge, the HPLC–MS/MS method reported herein is unique in its application to monitor modifications in vegetable polyglutamyl 5-methyltetrahydrofolate. ... Our finding is consistent with McKillop et al. who found no significant loss of folate after steaming spinach and broccoli for 4.5 and 15.0 min, respectively [[ref-type: bibr] 30]. ... Thus, steaming could obviate the need for the di-enzyme treatment, leaving only the conjugase step for folate species determination. ... Such fundamental information can aid researchers in several fields including plant biochemists, nutritionists and food scientists aiming to modulate polyglutamyl folate profiles.\n### 3.5. Folate speciation\n\nThe total 5MTHF folate contents we found are in line with literature values [[ref-type: bibr] 4 [Source reference: [Source link 45]],[ref-type: bibr] 5 [Source reference: [Source link 46]],[ref-type: bibr] 32 [Source reference: [Source link 47]]–[ref-type: bibr] 37 [Source reference: [Source link 48]]] although our polyglutamate profiles are in most comparisons distinctly shifted towards longer chain species. Readers should keep in mind that total 5MTHF is not equal to total folate. From our analysis, 5MTHF was the major form of folate in most of our vegetables (67–77% for non-leafy Brassicaceae, 56% for Amaranthaceae and 51–64% for Asteraceae) with 5-formyltetrahydrofolate (5CHOTHF) as the second major form (16–27% for non-leafy Brassicaceae, 21–30% for Amaranthaceae and 20–22% for Asteraceae). However, broccoli rabe had a similar proportion of 5MTHF (47%) and 5CHOTHF (43%) while unexpectedly the four leafy green vegetables belonging to the Brassicaceae family (collard greens, kale, bok choy and mustard greens) had a predominance of 5CHOTHF at 42–59%. For these four vegetables, 5MTHF species were secondary at 28–37%. Also of note, Swiss chard and escarole had 20% and 26%, respectively as 10-formyldihydrofolate (10CHODHF) and dandelion greens had 22% as 10-formylfolic acid (10CHOFA). Konings et al. also found significant contribution of 10CHOFA and 10CHODHF to total folate in many vegetables [[ref-type: bibr] 4 [Source reference: [Source link 49]]]. As folate species in these vegetables had not been characterized previously, this information expands the folate knowledge base.\n\n[sec-type: conclusions]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpen Access CAAS Agricultural Journals: Determination of folates in vegetables and their retention during boiling (https://www.agriculturejournals.cz/artkey/cjf-200801-0004_determination-of-folates-in-vegetables-and-their-retention-during-boiling.php)\nciteturn551search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; HPLC method for 5-methyltetrahydrofolate (5-MTHF) determination in vegetables was optimised for the folate release from the food matrix. ... Spinach, Chinese cabbage, lettuce, cauliflower, and broccoli contained more than 50 μg of 5-MTHF/100g, whereas less than 25 μg/100 g was found in potatoes, carrot, white cabbage, green and yellow pepper.\n\nCzech J. Food Sci., 2008, 26(1):31-37 | DOI: 10.17221/1137-CJFS\n\n## Determination of folates in vegetables and their retention during boiling\n\nMarie HOLASOVÁ, Vlasta FIEDLEROVÁ, Slavomíra VAVREINOVÁ\n    Food Research Institute Prague, Prague, Czech Republic\n\nHPLC method for 5-methyltetrahydrofolate (5-MTHF) determination in vegetables was optimised for the folate release from the food matrix. Enzymatic hydrolyses using the subsequent addition of α-amylase, protease, and conjugase from hog kidney, or their combinations, were tested. The highest release values were obtained with the application of enzymes α-amylase and conjugase, amounting to 112.4-127.0% of the values obtained in the processing with sole conjugase. The simultaneous addition of both enzymes and the incubation at pH 4.9 did not suppress the release of folates. Spinach, Chinese cabbage, lettuce, cauliflower, and broccoli contained more than 50 μg of 5-MTHF/100g, whereas less than 25 μg/100 g was found in potatoes, carrot, white cabbage, green and yellow pepper. Individual vegetables differed in the folate retention during their boiling under constant conditions. The highest retention was found in Brussels sprouts, cauliflower, and broccoli. After 8 min boiling more than 75% of the initial amount of 5-MTHF remained in these vegetables. Lower values of 5-MTHF retention, between 37% and 52% of their initial content, were found in spinach, savoy cabbage, and carrot.\n\n##### Keywords: vegetables; folate; determination; retention\n\nPublished: February 29, 2008  Show citation\n\nACS  | AIP  | APA  | ASA  | Harvard  | Chicago  | Chicago Notes  | IEEE  | ISO690  | MLA  | NLM  | Turabian  | Vancouver\n\nHOLASOVÁ M, FIEDLEROVÁ V, VAVREINOVÁ S. Determination of folates in vegetables and their retention during boiling. Czech Journal of Food Sciences. 2008;26(1):31-37. doi: 10.17221/1137-CJFS.\n\n\n | Open full article\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndoi:10.1016/j.foodchem.2004.08.007 (https://www.ars.usda.gov/ARSUserFiles/80400525/Articles/FoodChem_92_587-595.pdf)\nciteturn551search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 21.6 years ago; (b) Title: BCR 485 - Lyophilized Mixed Vegetables ... Results for canned spinach control 