afy green providing vitamin C, vitamin K, calcium and carotenoids\n\nImage\n\nSpinach\n\nLeafy green rich in iron, magnesium, folate, and carotenoids\n\nImage\n\nSwiss Chard\n\nLeafy green rich in iron, magnesium, folate, and carotenoids\n\n## Biological Regulatory Systems​\n\nBiological Regulatory System  | Evidence-qualified relationship  | Evidence\n--- | --- | ---\nMethylation & One-Carbon Metabolism (BRS2)  | 5-MTHF is the methyl-donor substrate for methionine synthase in folate- and B12-dependent remethylation. Other dietary folates can be converted to it. Necessity of the substrate does not show that raising intake increases tissue flux.  | [2]\n\n## References​\n\n[1] Wang, Riedl and Schwartz (2010). A liquid chromatography–tandem mass spectrometric method for quantitative determination of native 5-methyltetrahydrofolate and its polyglutamyl derivatives in raw vegetables. Identifies polyglutamyl 5-MTHF as a principal native folate in the store-bought vegetables studied, including the five foods linked on this page. Source-specific calculated amounts are retained on the canonical food records, expressed as 5-MTHF equivalents; they are not total-folate values.\n\n[2] Froese, Fowler and Baumgartner (2019). Vitamin B12, folate, and the methionine remethylation cycle—biochemistry, pathways, and regulation. Describes 5-MTHF as the methyl donor for methionine synthase, and the conversion of other dietary folate forms to that substrate through the folate cycle.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDetermination of folates in vegetables and their retention during boiling (https://cjfs.agriculturejournals.cz/pdfs/cjf/2008/01/04.pdf)\nciteturn551search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.7 years ago; Crawled: 18.7 years ago; Food Sci. ... the trienzyme method under the conditions tested needed 8 h of incubation. ... An optimised method was used for the folate determination in the selected vegetables that represent significant f